  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable temperatures and dry this week.\n\nLow pressure is centered off the coast of southern New England \nearly this morning, with a trailing cold front off the Mid- \nAtlantic coast. Meanwhile, a narrow ridge of surface high \npressure is located over the Piedmont. Widespread stratus covers\nmuch of the region early this morning, with some fog possible \nover the Piedmont toward sunrise. A shortwave trough will track \nacross the Northeast early this morning, which will allow for \nlow pressure to lift to the NE and the cold front to move \nfarther offshore. This will allow drier air to filter in from \nthe N with stratus lifting and scattering later this morning. \nBecoming mostly sunny later this morning through the afternoon \n(some high clouds may linger SE) with a light N wind. Forecast \nhighs are in the mid to upper 70s, which are a few degrees above\nNBM, noting that statistical guidance shows highs in the upper \n70s to around 80F.\n\nA dry cold front slides N-S across the Mid-Atlantic tonight as \nhigh pressure builds SE from the Great Lakes. Low temperatures \nlikely drop into the mid/upper 40s from the Piedmont to the MD \nEastern Shore ranging to the mid/upper 50s across coastal \nregions of SE VA and NE NC. High temperatures Tuesday will be \nslightly below average and generally in the mid 60s to lower \n70s. Tuesday night will be the coolest of the week (and of the \nseason so far) with widespread lows in the 40s and 50s at the \nimmediate coast as high pressure settles over the region. Some \nupper 30s are possible in rural and typically cooler locations \nin the Piedmont. High pressure shifts offshore Wednesday with \nhigh temperatures moderating back to near average, which are in \nthe lower to mid 70s. The high shifts farther offshore Thursday \nwith high temperatures rising into the upper 70s to lower 80s \n(potentially mid 80s away from the coast) ahead of another dry \ncold front. This cold front crosses the region Thursday night \nwith temperatures trending down to near seasonal averages Friday\nand Saturday.\n\nA very dry airmass arrives behind the cold front tonight. \n00z/05 EPS/GEFS/GEPS ensemble guidance showing PW values falling\nto 35-50% of normal Tuesday and only 'recovering' to 50-70% \nWednesday. A slight increase to 70-90% of normal is expected by \nThursday ahead of the cold front, and then the airmass behind \nthe front has a similar (lack) of moisture. Overall, dry \nconditions will prevail at least through Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Moisture does not return until late this \nweekend into early next week.\n\nBy late this weekend into early next week, there is a potential\nthat moisture gets pulled northward from the western Gulf and \nthen pushed eastward across the region ahead of a trough over \nthe mid- Mississippi Valley. Confidence is definitely low with \nregard to details. However, global ensemble guidance does show \nPW values rising above normal, with some deeper moisture in the \n700-300mb layer. Given this, a chc for showers returns to the \nforecast Sunday and Monday. Temperatures should remain near \naverage late this weekend and early next week.",
